{
  "id": "shadow_umbrella_plan",
  "duration": 120,
  "fps": 12,
  "hook_end": 2,
  "narration": "My shadow walked home without me. At first, I laughed. It waved across the street. I waved. It didn't. It pointed at my umbrella. I'd left it at the café. I ran back inside. The cashier looked terrified. My shadow was already there. It pointed at the clock. The hands ran backwards. I grabbed my umbrella. The sky turned orange. My friend laughed at me. His shadow shook its head. We followed both shadows. They found a blue door. A key lay underneath. My friend picked it up. Inside was our classroom. Every desk was empty. Except one by the window. A note sat there. Remember the clock, it said. I'd seen it at the café. The handwriting was mine. Night had arrived outside. My shadow faced the moon. It held out a hand. I opened the umbrella. A streetlamp cast another shadow. That one waved properly. The first smiled back. It pointed toward home. We walked home together. He carried the note. We reached my porch. The clock read six. When I'd left earlier. The umbrella glowed yellow. I folded it and entered. The hallway seemed normal. My friend knocked outside. His shadow had vanished. I looked at my feet. Two shadows stood there. One pointed at the clock. One reached for the umbrella. I understood the warning. The clock wasn't going backwards. It counted down to sunset. The umbrella kept shadows together. I opened it again. His shadow returned. He laughed and ran home. I put it beside my bed. Morning felt perfectly normal. Until I checked the clock. It was stuck at six. My umbrella was already gone.",
